Hi,
This is my first post in here and I've just browsed through the FAQ: I'm a
visually impaired computer enthusiast from Finland. Although I like Nethack,
I've discovered it is pretty difficult to play with Windows based screen
reader programs (ideally I'd like to have magnification tracking my
character and prompts via speech). In the console port, you have to use a
virtual cursor all the time and in the GUI port the messages don't get focus
automatically (heck, even modal dialogs would be better regarding
accessibility).

Anyway these practical difficulties in playing made me thought of an
alternative version of Nethack that would be even less graphical and more
accessible. That is howabout turning it into an interactive fiction game?

User interaction would not need to be that fancy a two or three word parser
would do provided that it could map textual user input into nethack input
commands (e.g. attack, examine, inventory etc...). Of course it would be
nice if there were some alternative prompts for the most common messages, so
that the interaction would feel slightly less monotonous than usual
(usually, you have the map to help you so it is not that bad).

The map could be described in room-sized chunks in a typical IF-fashion.
That is giving fuzzy info on room size, shape and type as well as listing
the monsters and other items. Naturally, the passages leading out of the
current room would be listed at the end.

I suppose moving would ordinarily take place from room to room or till the
next turn in a corridor that is provided that you don't encounter any
monsters, traps or other obstacles on the way. I'm not sure how battles
would be implemented, but the user would have to fight every turn just like
in the original game.

I also wonder how difficult it would be to implement such an nh2if mapping.
I know some CPP and Java but really wouldn't want to start working on this
on my own as I don't know the Nethack source nor do I know any programming
language that is especially designed for intereactive fiction. is anyone
else possibly interested?

I guess initial development comes down to how well an if-game language and
the Nethack engine work together. How object-oriented (OOPy) is the Nethack
code and does it follow the model view controller (MVC) design pattern?

Ideally, one would run the Nethack engine along side an interactive fiction
gamehandling user input and output in the IF-part and asking stuff from the
engine. An example function in the game could be something like nhEngine,
give me a Vector of Attackable Actors located in this Room, and check  if
the last word of the user input matches with any of the verbal names of the
Objects. If it does, return the matching Object as a baseclass (Monster)
pointer and null otherwise. Of course I don't know how these things are
named or handled in Nethack source, this is just an imaginary example.

Things will be very difficult if the nethack source is not OOPY and if the
map and user input are tightly coupled with the engine. also, I don't know
if any IF-languages support C or CPP directly. in the extreme nnightmare
scenario, one would have to port the whole of nethack apart from the map and
the user input into Tads or some other interactive fiction language. My
original idea was that this could be a rather quick and easy conversion
given no engine porting was necessary.

Lastly, is this the right group to ask about Nethack development and
howabout the YANI tag, is it correct form? I threw in a question mark in the
end as I was a bit uncertain.

